    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d722">

                <pb facs="0879" n="791" xml:id="pg791"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">338.1121 Zrike Bros. and Johnson/24:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Haiti</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Port-au-Prince</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-04-21T11:00:00-05:00">April 21, 1933—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 1:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>47. Reference Legation’s telegram No. 39, April 7, 9 p.m.; despatch No. 11 of
                    April 7.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d722-f1"/> The Hudicourt resolution referred to
                    therein was adopted yesterday by a [vote] of 18 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >12?</hi>] to 7 in the Senate after a heated discussion which lasted several
                    hours. The debate in support of the resolution was led by Senator Pradel in the
                    course of which he severely criticised the American administration in Haiti. A
                    number of the Senators took the position that they did not favor an immediate
                    vote but swung over to vote with the majority. It is not known whether the
                    Government attempted to exercise any pressure to prevent passage of this
                    resolution.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
